<meta type=article>
  <title | Rendering guide>
  <author | The Enscribe Project>
</meta>

<config toc toc-location=right />

How an Enscribe document becomes the thing a reader opens is three independent choices, not one. This guide
names the three dimensions, gives a yes/no path through the rendering choice, and lists every `<config>`
option that tunes the output — the option grid below is **generated from the engine's own option set**, so it
can't drift from what the engine actually accepts.

<# #sec:dimensions | The three dimensions #>

A document is described along three axes that vary independently. Pick each one separately.

<## Rendering — how the HTML is produced ##>

- **Precompiled** — the engine compiles the source to static HTML ahead of time. Deploy the files anywhere,
  no server, no JavaScript required to read; the output is frozen at build time.
- **Live on a server** — `enscribe serve` renders the `.emd` on each request, so the page is always fresh
  from the current source; it needs a running server.
- **Live, client-side** — ship the `.emd` plus a small HTML shell; the browser fetches the source and renders
  it. Static-host-able like the precompiled form, but the `.emd` stays the source of truth (the rendered HTML
  is never committed).

<## File structure — how the source is organised ##>

- **Single file** — the whole document in one `.emd`.
- **Multiple files via `src=`** — a spectrum, not a binary: from a mostly-inline file that pulls in a section
  or two (`<section src="methods.emd">`), through a skeleton **master** that pulls in everything (the
  multi-file assembler). The same `src=` mechanism spans the whole range.

<## Editing — how the source is authored ##>

- **Author `.emd` by hand** — the shorthand authoring path; what nearly everyone does.
- **Author Layer 1 by hand** — write the canonical HTML directly; a rare escape hatch.
- **In-browser editing** — edit `.emd` and re-render live in the browser: the live app-shell's
  Write/Preview edit loop, which re-renders only the edited chapter (#203 / #216). *(Shipped,
  preview-only — edits live in memory; save / write-back is the named follow-on.)*

<## These axes are orthogonal ##>

The three choices don't constrain each other, and **document type — article, book, or website — gates none of
them.** An article can be multi-file; a book can be a single file; any type can be precompiled, served live,
or rendered client-side. Choose each axis for its own reasons.

| Dimension | Options |
|---|---|
| Rendering | precompiled · live on server · live client-side |
| File structure | single file · multiple files via `src=` |
| Editing | author `.emd` · author Layer 1 · in-browser (live edit loop, preview-only) |
| Document type *(orthogonal)* | article · book · website |

<# #sec:choosing | Choosing how to render #>

The rendering axis is the one with a real decision behind it. Walk it as a yes/no:

<diagram mermaid |
flowchart TD
  A{Do you run a server?} -->|yes| B[Live on server<br/>always fresh from source]
  A -->|no| C{Need frozen, no-JS HTML<br/>that deploys anywhere?}
  C -->|yes| D[Precompiled<br/>static build]
  C -->|no| E[Live, client-side<br/>ship .emd + a shell]
>

In words: if you run a server, **live on server** keeps the page in lockstep with the source. If not and you
need self-contained, no-JavaScript HTML, **precompile**. Otherwise **live client-side** keeps the `.emd` as the
source while still hosting statically.

<# #sec:options | The `<config>` options #>

Every knob below is set with a `<config>` block — `<config number-sections toc-location=right>`. The grid is
generated from the engine's `<config>` allowlist and its documentation source, grouped by family. Boolean
options take no value (a bare `<config toc>` means `toc=true`); valued options list their accepted values.
**book-only** options apply only to a `<meta type=book>` document, and **website-only** only to a
`<meta type=website>`.

<sub-section #cfg-fam-numbering | Numbering>

| option | what it does | type / values | default | scope |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `number-equations` | Number display equations document-wide. Set false to suppress the equation counter. | boolean | on | all |
| `number-figures` | Number figures. Set false to suppress the figure counter. | boolean | on | all |
| `number-tables` | Number tables. Set false to suppress the table counter. | boolean | on | all |
| `number-theorems` | Number the theorem family — theorem / lemma / corollary / proposition share one counter. | boolean | on | all |
| `number-definitions` | Number definitions. | boolean | on | all |
| `number-examples` | Number examples. | boolean | on | all |
| `number-boxes` | Number boxes (numbered &lt;aside&gt;). Boxes are UNnumbered by default — opt a box in with +numbered. | boolean | off | all |
| `number-sections` | Hierarchical heading numbering (1, 1.1, 1.1.1). Off by default for ALL document types (#246/core) — opt in with &lt;config number-sections&gt;. A &lt;ref&gt; to an unnumbered heading shows its title. | boolean | off | all |
| `number-depth` | Deepest heading level that receives a number. Independent of toc-depth — a heading can be numbered but not listed, or vice versa. | valued · a non-negative integer (heading level) | all levels | all |
| `counter-reset-scope` | Where float / equation / theorem counters reset. none = global; chapter = at each &lt;book-part&gt;; section = also at each outermost &lt;section&gt;. | valued · none \| chapter \| section | none in articles, chapter in books | all |
| `ref-prefix-<type>` | Override the cross-reference label prefix for a float type — a wildcard pattern (e.g. ref-prefix-eqn, ref-prefix-tbl), not a fixed key. | valued · any text (e.g. ref-prefix-fig = "Fig.") | the type’s built-in prefix | all |

<sub-section #cfg-fam-table-of-contents | Table of contents>

| option | what it does | type / values | default | scope |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `toc` | Render the config-driven contents listing (read in the shared compiler, so static and live agree). | boolean | off | all |
| `toc-depth` | Deepest heading level shown in the listing. | valued · a positive integer | 3 | all |
| `toc-title` | Heading shown above the listing. | valued · any text | Contents | all |
| `toc-location` | Where the listing sits: body = inline at the top of the document; left / right = a sticky sidebar. | valued · body \| left \| right | body | all |
| `toc-expand` | How many sidebar levels are expanded initially (sidebar locations only). | valued · an integer, or all \| none | 1 | all |

<sub-section #cfg-fam-citations-bibliography | Citations & bibliography>

| option | what it does | type / values | default | scope |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `citation-style` | The bibliography and in-text citation style. | valued · a citation-js / CSL style name (e.g. apa, vancouver, ieee) | chicago-author-date | all |
| `bibliography-heading` | Overrides the bibliography’s default "References" heading. | valued · any text | References | all |
| `bibliography-position` *(reserved)* | Reserved — intended to control where the bibliography sits in the document. No consumer yet. | valued | — | all |

<sub-section #cfg-fam-notes | Notes>

| option | what it does | type / values | default | scope |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `note-scope` | Where footnotes collect: document = one back-matter list; chapter = per &lt;book-part&gt;; section = per outermost &lt;section&gt;. | valued · document \| chapter \| section | section in articles, chapter in books | all |
| `note-position` | Render position of numbered notes. bottom = foot of the document; margin = Tufte-style sidenotes in a wide margin column. | valued · bottom \| margin | bottom | all |

<sub-section #cfg-fam-book-navigation | Book navigation>

| option | what it does | type / values | default | scope |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `chapter-nav` | The persistent chapter rail (its header is the return-to-cover link). | boolean | on | book-only |
| `chapter-nav-depth` | How deep the chapter rail lists. | valued · 1 = chapters; &gt;=2 = chapters + their sections | 1 | book-only |
| `page-navigation` | Prev / next chapter links at the foot of each chapter. | boolean | on | book-only |
| `cover` | Show the cover / title page as the landing view. Off = land on the first chapter. | boolean | on | book-only |
| `back-to-top` | An optional scroll-to-top control within a chapter. | boolean | off | book-only |
| `on-this-page` | The per-chapter "on this page" rail (the current chapter’s section list, right column). Off drops the reading interface to two columns. | boolean | on | book-only |
| `split-by` | The pagination unit. Only chapter is built; section and none are specified but deferred. | valued · chapter \| section \| none | chapter | book-only |

<sub-section #cfg-fam-website-navigation | Website navigation>

| option | what it does | type / values | default | scope |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `sidebar` | The left navigation sidebar (the full nav tree). The sticky top bar is a website’s primary nav; the sidebar is an opt-in second surface — off by default, and the layout reflows to give the content the freed column. | boolean | off | website-only |

<sub-section #cfg-fam-display-dsl-strict | Display / DSL / strict>

| option | what it does | type / values | default | scope |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `theme` | Inject a theme’s :root token overrides after the base stylesheet. default (or unset) injects nothing. | valued · default \| modern \| compact | default | all |
| `display-style` *(reserved)* | Reserved — no consumer yet. | valued | — | all |
| `show-source` | Reveal the authored DSL source behind a rendered diagram (mermaid / abc) in a native &lt;details&gt; disclosure. | boolean | off | all |
| `parse-data-tables` | Document-wide default for whether DATA-format table cells (&lt;table csv\|json\|…&gt;) parse as Enscribe inline markup. Per-table attributes override it. | boolean | off | all |
| `strict-mode` | The strictness register switch. off = all registers interpret; sigil = the markdown register is off; canonical = markdown AND sigils are off (only canonical tags). | valued · off \| sigil \| canonical | off | all |
| `quiet` | Suppress THIS document’s authoring warnings (raw-HTML passthrough, mis-placed apparatus, …) from build/console output. Page-scoped — a quiet page hushes while other pages still warn. Gates emission only; rendering and the inline error markers are unchanged. The supported way to quiet teaching/demo pages that deliberately show warnings-worthy markup. | boolean | off | all |

